67694091796875, 0.0125579833984375, -0.0025539398193359375, -0.0036144256591796875, 0.01488494873046875, 0.050750732421875, 0.037353515625, 0.033447265625, 0.0174102783203125, 0.00853729248046875, -0.006256103515625, -0.042816162109375, 0.00830078125, -0.0006799697875976562, 0.000396728515625, -0.030059814453125, -0.002231597900390625, 0.00955963134765625, -0.007625579833984375, -0.034942626953125, 0.00011873245239257812, -0.0182342529296875, 0.006336212158203125, -0.00974273681640625, -0.02325439453125, -0.02447509765625, 0.01010894775390625, -0.0038738250732421875, -0.0211029052734375, 0.0251007080078125, -0.0200347900390625, 0.029449462890625, 0.0080718994140625, -0.056304931640625, -0.032257080078125, 0.00026702880859375, 0.0183563232421875, -0.0265350341796875, 0.002574920654296875, 0.03106689453125, 0.0019388198852539062, 0.0133056640625, -0.0259552001953125, -0.01331329345703125, 0.03955078125, -0.0163116455078125, 0.0127716064453125, 0.004253387451171875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\nRecent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, with particular attention to electrical circuits. However, a central challenge remains: the transformation of the ordinary differential equations governing a system's behavior from the real domain to the Laplace domain, a process that is well-established in classical analysis but not yet fully developed within the framework of GA. While previous works have extended the Laplace transform to certain hypercomplex algebras\u2014such as quaternions [1] and Clifford algebras [2,3]\u2014a comprehensive definition and analysis within the broader context of geometric algebras remains lacking. Specifically, prior studies have either focused on special cases or have not addressed the full range of multivector-valued functions encountered in GA-based modeling.\n\nThe specific research question addressed in this work is: How can the Laplace transform be rigorously defined and systematically applied to multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras, and what properties does this transform preserve relative to the classical case? Our novel contribution is the introduction of a general definition of the Laplace transform for geometric algebras of arbitrary signature (p, q) with p + q \u2264 5, together with a detailed analysis of its properties and application to practical systems. This extends previous results by providing a unified framework that accommodates both scalar and multivector components, enabling the direct use of GA in the analysis of dynamical systems.\n\nWe restrict our attention to geometric algebras with signature (p, q) where p + q \u2264 5. This choice is motivated by both practical and theoretical considerations. Algebras of this size include those most commonly used in physics and engineering, such as the algebra of physical space (Cl(3,0)), spacetime algebra (Cl(1,3)), and their duals. These algebras are computationally tractable and avoid the combinatorial explosion and potential algebraic pathologies (such as non-simple ideals and nontrivial center structures) that arise in higher dimensions [4]. Moreover, the majority of applications in engineering and physics do not require higher-dimensional algebras, so this restriction does not significantly limit the generality of our results.\n\nThe remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In Section 1, we review related work on Laplace transforms in hypercomplex and Clifford algebras, highlighting the limitations of existing approaches. Section 2 introduces our formal definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras, together with the main existence and linearity theorems. Section 3 demonstrates the preservation of key properties, such as linearity, the treatment of derivatives (i.e., the Laplace transform of the derivative corresponds to multiplication by the Laplace variable, with initial conditions encoded as multivectors), and the handling of initial conditions. Section 4 presents an illustrative example: the analysis of an RLC circuit modeled within GA, emphasizing the unification of scalar and multivector components and the simplification of the solution process. We conclude in Section 5 with a discussion of future research directions and potential applications.\n\nOur formal definition of the Laplace transform in GA is as follows: For a multivector-valued function \\( f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\), where \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) is a geometric algebra of signature (p, q), the Laplace transform is defined by\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f](s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is a real or complex parameter and the exponential is interpreted componentwise. We prove that this transform is linear, i.e., \\( \\mathcal{L}[af + bg] = a\\mathcal{L}[f] + b\\mathcal{L}[g] \\) for scalars \\( a, b \\), and that it exists for functions \\( f \\) that are piecewise continuous and of exponential order, analogous to the classical case. Furthermore, we establish that the Laplace transform of the derivative satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f'](s) = s\\mathcal{L}[f](s) - f(0),\n\\]\nwhere \\( f(0) \\) is a multivector initial condition, thus preserving the classical operational property.\n\nIn our illustrative example, we analyze an RLC circuit where voltages and currents are represented as multivector-valued functions to capture both scalar and oriented quantities (such as flux and current loops) within a unified GA framework. The Laplace transform is applied to the system's differential equations, and the resulting algebraic equations are solved directly in the geometric algebra, demonstrating how our approach streamlines the analysis by avoiding the need to decompose the system into scalar and vector parts. This example highlights the practical advantage of our method in unifying the treatment of all circuit quantities and simplifying the solution process.\n\nIn summary, our results provide a foundation for further development of analytical and computational tools for dynamical systems in geometric algebra. Future work could extend the framework to higher-dimensional algebras, explore numerical algorithms for Laplace-domain computations in GA, and apply the method to more complex systems in electromagnetics, robotics, and quantum mechanics.\n\nReferences:\n[1] S. L. Adler, Quaternionic Quantum Mechanics and Quantum Fields, Oxford University Press, 1995.\n[2] J. Helmstetter and A. Micali, Quadratic Mappings and Clifford Algebras, Birkh\u00e4user, 1982.\n[3] D. Hestenes and G. Sobczyk, Clifford Algebra to Geometric Calculus, Reidel, 1984.\n[4] P. Lounesto, Clifford Algebras and Spinors, Cambridge University Press, 2001.\n\"\"\""}}